# !!! NOTE ON FORMAT STRINGS !!!
# As of version 3.5.3, Password Tech uses format specifiers of the form "%N",
# with N being the index of the argument (starting at N=1) to be inserted into
# the formatted string. All arguments are provided as strings by the application.
# The new format was chosen because it provides higher flexibility and security.
# Example:
# Format string:    "%1 characters / %2 bits per character"
# 1st argument:     "90"
# 2nd argument:     "6.4"
# Resulting string: "90 characters / 6.4 bits per character"
# In the translated string, the arguments can be specified in any order.
# Password Tech is still compatible with the old format ("%d" for digits, "%f"
# for floating point numbers, "%s" for strings, etc.), and the original strings
# are still provided in the old format to avoid merge conflicts when updating
# existing translations.
